[[:db/cas 17592186443990 :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =8,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(8Δx)=1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.6.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =8,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(8Δx)=1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle |32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.6.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444007 :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{16}y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" "Given the equation $$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{16}y=0\n$$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?"] [:db/cas 17592186444062 :question/problem "Given that $P(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle P(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186444063 :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186444064 :question/problem "Given that $P(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle P(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186444147 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/665ff96d191a9b2dc6dc57f1c3a97743dcb1a26c4118151716bdf430a4c014f3.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/665ff96d191a9b2dc6dc57f1c3a97743dcb1a26c4118151716bdf430a4c014f3.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186444148 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186444149 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2c81ff3c50013c149dc6a5db67bbe11cccdbbef22ce2c0712101f4a358bcb8c.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{2t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{2t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{t})^{′}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}}{{c}_{1}{e}^{t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2c81ff3c50013c149dc6a5db67bbe11cccdbbef22ce2c0712101f4a358bcb8c.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{2t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{2t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{t})^{′}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}}{{c}_{1}{e}^{t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186444364 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =4$ and $\\lambda =5$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =4$ and $\\lambda =5$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444364 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186444366 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444366 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186444534 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & -2y \\\\ -2x & -{e}^{y}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & -2y \\\\ -2x & -{e}^{y}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444536 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-2x+3y+3 \\\\ -4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2)-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4(-1)-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-2x+3y+3 \\\\ -4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2)-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4(-1)-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186444537 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+y-5 \\\\ {y}^{2}+x-3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-5)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-5)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+x-3)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(2,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(2) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+y-5 \\\\ {y}^{2}+x-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-5)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-5)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+x-3)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(2,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(2) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444538 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y+(x-2)^{3}+8 \\\\ -2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+(x-2)^{3}+8)=3(x-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8))=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}3(x-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-8]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}3((2)-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8+8)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y+(x-2)^{3}+8 \\\\ -2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+(x-2)^{3}+8)=3(x-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8))=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}3(x-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-8]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}3((2)-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8+8)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}i.\n$$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444539 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & \\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+(0)^{2}} & 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=3,\\text{ }5.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues,, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & \\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+(0)^{2}} & 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=3,\\text{ }5.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues,, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444540 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+y-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-1)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-1)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=\\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+3} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -\\frac{1}{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{6}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+y-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-1)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-1)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=\\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+3} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -\\frac{1}{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444542 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+y-4x+4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=2x+3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=-2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3 & -2y+2 \\\\ 2x-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+3 & -2(-1)+2 \\\\ 2(1)-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+y-4x+4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=2x+3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=-2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3 & -2y+2 \\\\ 2x-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+3 & -2(-1)+2 \\\\ 2(1)-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186444543 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+{y}^{2} \\\\ -2y+{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3x+{y}^{2})=-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+{x}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+{x}^{2})=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2,\\text{ }-3.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real and negative eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+{y}^{2} \\\\ -2y+{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3x+{y}^{2})=-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+{x}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+{x}^{2})=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2,\\text{ }-3.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real and negative eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186444768 :question/problem "What is the constant term in the expression $\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{4}{9}-w+\\frac{1}{5}x?$" "What is the constant term in the expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{4}{9}-w+\\frac{1}{5}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186444838 :question/problem "What is  $\\text{ }-\\frac{1}{11}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{11}\\right)?$" "What is  $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{ }-\\frac{1}{11}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{11}\\right)?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186444897 :question/problem "![](images/12/12acb33aa174f1c174a538a89881dd19245f733c6b39df37f9ebbdc1f86442d5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-3,1]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" "![](images/12/12acb33aa174f1c174a538a89881dd19245f733c6b39df37f9ebbdc1f86442d5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-3,1]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186445183 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3},\\,{f}^{′}(-1)=\\sqrt{3},\\,g(-1)=-3,\\,{g}^{′}(-1)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=-1.$ Do not round the answer. \n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3},\\,{f}^{′}(-1)=\\sqrt{3},\\,g(-1)=-3,\\,{g}^{′}(-1)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=-1.$ Do not round the answer. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=-1}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186445189 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(0)=3,\\,{f}^{′}(0)=2,\\,g(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{g}^{′}(0)=-1\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=0.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=0}=$\n{{field-1}}" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(0)=3,\\,{f}^{′}(0)=2,\\,g(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{g}^{′}(0)=-1\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=0.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=0}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186445322 :question/worked-solution "The tablecloth will have an area of $(2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{2}){\\text{m}}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(1\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{9\\times 3}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{8}=3\\,\\text{R}3=3\\,\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$" "The tablecloth will have an area of $$\n\\displaystyle (2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{2}){\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(1\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{9\\times 3}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{8}=3\\,\\text{R}3=3\\,\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186445484 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}{y}^{2}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,3].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}{y}^{2}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $$\n\\displaystyle R=[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,3].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186445488 :ques