t)\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 3\\pi $ ?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2{cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 3\\pi $ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186447158 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ ?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186447159 :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b8ef36c29d1babc5c3ccf1d474678a18ed5937b883e026d18d5c7ea1aaaa3f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" "![](images/3b/3b8ef36c29d1babc5c3ccf1d474678a18ed5937b883e026d18d5c7ea1aaaa3f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186447160 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\pi /3\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\pi /3\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2?$"] [:db/cas 17592186447465 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447470 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 6 & 30 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 0 \\\\ -80 & 14 & 60\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 6 & 30 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 0 \\\\ -80 & 14 & 60\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447472 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447473 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447629 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,2,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,2,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -1-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y}{2}=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t\\,\\,y=2t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t+2(-t)+3(-t)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -4t+3{t}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(1)=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(-1,2,-1).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,2,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,2,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -1-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y}{2}=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t\\,\\,y=2t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t+2(-t)+3(-t)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -4t+3{t}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(1)=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(-1,2,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447634 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-z,3{y}^{2},-x⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-t)-3(-t)^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ t-3{t}^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ 2t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(2-3t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-z,3{y}^{2},-x⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-t)-3(-t)^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ t-3{t}^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ 2t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(2-3t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447640 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447641 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455436 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186455441 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186455443 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186455449 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(2)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(2)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(2)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(2)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455452 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186455453 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455453 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(-1)}{2}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(-1)}{2}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455454 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x+3y \\\\ -12x-9y\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" "Consider the linear transformation \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x+3y \\\\ -12x-9y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186455460 :question/problem "If \n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\text{rank}(F).$" "If \n$$\n\\displaystyle F=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\text{rank}(F).$"] [:db/cas 17592186455463 :question/problem "![](images/13/13e4c6a06a44099024ce91ef6c9ed4679af037c2e794351597a4d604bf39dc6f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" "![](images/13/13e4c6a06a44099024ce91ef6c9ed4679af037c2e794351597a4d604bf39dc6f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455463 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/63/6371bb04d33719ee18c65a92354e84be0e9077e780eac9559dcb7ec42deb8d99.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/0b/0bd9476fa9b19f04b40fbb9cca2986ed90495aa134307606622ef91070af19a7.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/63/6371bb04d33719ee18c65a92354e84be0e9077e780eac9559dcb7ec42deb8d99.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/0b/0bd9476fa9b19f04b40fbb9cca2986ed90495aa134307606622ef91070af19a7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186455471 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455471 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455484 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{x}y\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{x}y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186455485 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1883ee3a3a99d43109bae2d2093aef35548dde9753cfb7dc19dfd77a104ec83.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" "![](images/a1/a1883ee3a3a99d43109bae2d2093aef35548dde9753cfb7dc19dfd77a104ec83.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455485 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/85/8595904122fac3431fc959b2fa4aa5a3062bd1bffd1a391a0861fa12f2498054.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1f31f2b6eefddedc46b8e845703904c0b4566963e64a644f7a4ab32bb7240c3.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/85/8595904122fac3431fc959b2fa4aa5a3062bd1bffd1a391a0861fa12f2498054.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1f31f2b6eefddedc46b8e845703904c0b4566963e64a644f7a4ab32bb7240c3.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186455487 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d042734a8c7483c54795cd6ec8527b43f398cb77487d054a36b27d704f27a318.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" "![](images/d0/d042734a8c7483c54795cd6ec8527b43f398cb77487d054a36b27d704f27a318.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455487 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f8268167bb50f9d03cb38a3d505fa6b40003e307b149356089f5b0dc3dd32110.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a83934aa58040b4eb2bb957d1d12caba676e306e41086480d064935eab9f1c59.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f8268167bb50f9d03cb38a3d505fa6b40003e307b149356089f5b0dc3dd32110.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a83934aa58040b4eb2bb957d1d12caba676e306e41086480d064935eab9f1c59.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186455490 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455490 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy(1-{x}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy(1-{x}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455497 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186455499 :question/problem "![](images/36/36ebce64036b7661c81aa6525c407f698375544532985175cc465ddc3ff4f5a8.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" "![](images/36/36ebce64036b7661c81aa6525c407f698375544532985175cc465ddc3ff4f5a8.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455499 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/c4/c498ddeae8ba4325a4a1dfc0840520c4718bb50a0600970405374dde933e1b1d.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/74/740429a29b5eb4b72bbfae11e08c9ac272e72e31d395b4dad2602beb4d4ba40a.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/c4/c498ddeae8ba4325a4a1dfc0840520c4718bb50a0600970405374dde933e1b1d.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/74/740429a29b5eb4b72bbfae11e08c9ac272e72e31d395b4dad2602beb4d4ba40a.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186455506 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x+2y \\\\ -9x-6y\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" "Consider the linear transformation \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x+2y \\\\ -9x-6y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186455507 :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 2w+x-3y+2z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ find its rank." "Given the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 2w+x-3y+2z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find its rank."] [:db/cas 17592186455508 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186455509 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186459214 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{2}{3}f(t)+\\frac{1}{2}g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨3,\\,6,\\,-3⟩$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{1}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,2,\\,10⟩.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{2}{3}f(t)+\\frac{1}{2}g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨3,\\,6,\\,-3⟩\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,2,\\,10⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186459223 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." "![](images/1f/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186459224 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-4,\\,-6,\\,4⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨9,\\,9,\\,-6⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-4,\\,-6,\\,4⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨9,\\,9,\\,-6⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186459225 :question/problem "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\,j+2\\pi \\,k$ \nand \n$c=2\\,i-\\,k.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\,j+2\\pi \\,k\n$$ \nand \n$c=2\\,i-\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186459233 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ In turn, there is $v\\ne 0$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ In turn, there is $v\\ne 0$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186459237 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}[4f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{3}{2},\\,-2⟩$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-1,\\,2⟩.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}[4f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{3}{2},\\,-2⟩\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-1,\\,2⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186459243 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{5}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-7⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-7⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186459244 :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [:db/cas 17592186459244 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$" "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$"] [:db/cas 17592186459245 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ which means  $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ which means  $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 1