61add19094df16e617ece8daf.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3},\\,$ $x=3-y,$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "![](images/9c/9c684a6eaf9961b441cb559656aa2689ed0f25f61add19094df16e617ece8daf.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3},\\,$ $x=3-y,$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186475966 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186475974 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x/2}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x/2}^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186476879 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c7e555ed139343a1a83e0b05bdb9a13be502271b76e9b9bbdf7ed5f3e869fcd0.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c[{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c{[-\\frac{2}{3}(x-y)^{3/2}]}_{y=x-{x}^{2}}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(-0+\\frac{2}{3}(x-x+{x}^{2})^{3/2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=6.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c7e555ed139343a1a83e0b05bdb9a13be502271b76e9b9bbdf7ed5f3e869fcd0.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c[{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c{[-\\frac{2}{3}(x-y)^{3/2}]}_{y=x-{x}^{2}}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(-0+\\frac{2}{3}(x-x+{x}^{2})^{3/2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186476909 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<Y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}).$" "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle P(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<Y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186476933 :question/problem "Simplify the polynomial $-\\frac{5x}{3}+4{x}^{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+3x.$" "Simplify the polynomial $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{5x}{3}+4{x}^{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+3x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186476959 :question/problem "Let $D=[1,e]\\times [1,e].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-y}{4e},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{xy},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" "Let $D=[1,e]\\times [1,e].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-y}{4e},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{xy},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186476959 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$  is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2({e}^{2}-1)y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[({e}^{2}-1){y}^{2}]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1)^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,e)=\\frac{2-e}{4e}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$  is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2({e}^{2}-1)y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[({e}^{2}-1){y}^{2}]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1)^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,e)=\\frac{2-e}{4e}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186476977 :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y<1-x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a4fce220150c53a70ca236843203460a7040cc1a1b5dfe664c6d45dd93ac1.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cy]}_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c(1-x)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y<1-x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a4fce220150c53a70ca236843203460a7040cc1a1b5dfe664c6d45dd93ac1.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cy]}_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c(1-x)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n