 eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{-t} \\\\ y &={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.72$ | $0$ | $0.30$ |\n| $y$ | $2.72$ | $1$ | $0.61$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/1f/1f1d0d467285e26c4fc2f609c70d80fbb88867b77cdeb32c2a829006cbccea8e.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f7413babbb41aff9a892593d73c257d01ff24f2441f169e6089773b23434a5ab.png)" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{-t} \\\\ y &={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.72$ | $0$ | $0.30$ |\n| $y$ | $2.72$ | $1$ | $0.61$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/1f/1f1d0d467285e26c4fc2f609c70d80fbb88867b77cdeb32c2a829006cbccea8e.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f7413babbb41aff9a892593d73c257d01ff24f2441f169e6089773b23434a5ab.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186478454 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186478456 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478464 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478530 :question/problem "$\\int 7sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 7sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478540 :question/problem "$\\int (2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+7\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+7\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478548 :question/problem "$\\int 4sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478554 :question/problem "$\\int (6\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (6\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479090 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =6, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-(6)+4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =6, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-(6)+4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479095 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-4, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-5-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-5-2(-4) \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =9+4{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\  & =9+4(-4)+3(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-4, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-5-2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-5-2(-4) \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =9+4{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2} \\\\  & =9+4(-4)+3(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479110 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=1 \\\\ 9{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=1$ and ${y}_{2}=-3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}\\cdot {y}_{1}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=1 \\\\ 9{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=1$ and ${y}_{2}=-3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{2}\\cdot {y}_{1}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479139 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =1-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =1-(3) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-3-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-3-2(3)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =1-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =1-(3) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-3-2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-3-2(3)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479162 :question/problem "$\\int -9{csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -9{csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479163 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int csc⁡x(4cot⁡x+8csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int csc⁡x(4cot⁡x+8csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479169 :question/problem "$\\int (4\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-1{csc}^{2}x+7\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-1{csc}^{2}x+7\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479181 :question/problem "$\\int (9\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+9\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (9\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+9\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479186 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int csc⁡x(4cot⁡x+5csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int csc⁡x(4cot⁡x+5csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] 