asoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-4}} both sides of the equation." "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-4}} both sides of the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186483367 :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},\n$$ shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186483373 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9ea317575efcb32d8234309e80fecfd4385c99dbe69dd9c33d3fa7d80313bb6.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." "![](images/b9/b9ea317575efcb32d8234309e80fecfd4385c99dbe69dd9c33d3fa7d80313bb6.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186483377 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\,k.\n$$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186483378 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ }x(y-1)\\,dy\\,dx=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ }x(y-1)\\,dy\\,dx=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483379 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186483386 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x-1,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x-1,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483393 :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)÷\\left(-\\frac{3}{10}\\right).$" "Determine the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)÷\\left(-\\frac{3}{10}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483398 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483398 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483406 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{2}}(y-1)\\,dx\\,dy=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{2}}(y-1)\\,dx\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483407 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}8x{y}^{3}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and below the $x$-axis." "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}8x{y}^{3}\\,dA,\\,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and below the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186483413 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3dbd6e26cb67b2f247387c30e03296f790c3f56c03779a7dda5ae5d0b081958.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=3-{y}^{3},\\,$ $x={y}^{3},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA=$" "![](images/c3/c3dbd6e26cb67b2f247387c30e03296f790c3f56c03779a7dda5ae5d0b081958.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=3-{y}^{3},\\,$ $x={y}^{3},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483418 :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into {{field-3}} ." "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into {{field-3}} ."] [:db/cas 17592186483419 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above." "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186483421 :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186483424 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=4$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,…,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =96,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+3+4=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(96)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4\\cdot 4-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(96)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=96+10+6=112.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=4$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,…,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =96,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+3+4=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(96)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4\\cdot 4-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(96)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=96+10+6=112.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483515 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x\\sqrt{x^{2} - 20}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nWork on an interval where $x^{2} - 20>0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\sqrt{x^{2} - 20}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $x^{2} - 20>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186483524 :question/problem "$\\int x\\left(- x^{2} + 11\\right)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\left(- x^{2} + 11\\right)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483548 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{(5-3x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{(5-3x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483549 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4}{(3-x)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{(3-x)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484194 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t \\\\ y &=t-3, \\\\ z &=t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t \\\\ y &=t-3, \\\\ z &=t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484199 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484204 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{13}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{13}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [:db/cas 17592186484211 :question/problem "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 205 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 205 \\\\ 202 & 203 & 204\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484215 :question/problem "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." "For the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}=z+4,\n$$ the section in the plane $\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186484223 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{2}=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{1}=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+5 \\\\ y &=-t \\\\ z &=t-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ ((2t+5)+1)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+((t-6)-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (2t+6)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(t-8)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+24t+36)+{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-16t+64) & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t+100 & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t & =0 \\\\ 2t(3t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y &=-(0)=0 \\\\ z &=(0)-6=-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,-6).$\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+5=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)=\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ z &=\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)-6=-\\frac{22}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).$" "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{2}=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{1}=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+5 \\\\ y &=-t \\\\ z &=t-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ ((2t+5)+1)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+((t-6)-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (2t+6)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(t-8)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+24t+36)+{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-16t+64) & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t+100 & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t & =0 \\\\ 2t(3t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y &=-(0)=0 \\\\ z &=(0)-6=-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,-6).$\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+5=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)=\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ z &=\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)-6=-\\frac{22}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,-6)$ and $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484233 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{y}^{2}-4y+4 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4({y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3 \\\\ 6z & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+9 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.$" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{y}^{2}-4y+4 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4({y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3 \\\\ 6z & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+9 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484234 :question/problem "Given that $H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(H).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(H).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484236 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+1 \\\\ y &=2t+2, \\\\ z &=t+6\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+1 \\\\ y &=2t+2, \\\\ z &=t+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484244 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t \\\\ y &=2t, \\\\ z &=-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1\n$$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t \\\\ y &=2t, \\\\ z &=-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484270 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t \\\\ y &=2-t, \\\\ z &=t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t \\\\ y &=2-t, \\\\ z &=t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484286 :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{aligned}|=5$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{aligned}|.$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a & b-1\\end{aligned}|=5,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ a+2 & b\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484293 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,z=2+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(2+y)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(4+4y+{y}^{2}) & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+1) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1.$" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,z=2+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(2+y)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(4+4y+{y}^{2}) & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+1) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484366 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{12x}.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{12x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484409 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle y=3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\n$$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484412 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle y=2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\n$$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484415 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle y=4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right).\n$$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484422 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0$. Inward and outward contributions cancel because $xy$ has zero average on the circle." "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0.\n$$ Inward and outward contributions cancel because $xy$ has zero average on the circle."] [:db/cas 17592186484434 :question/worked-solution "The speed is $2\\sqrt2\\,t$ on $0\\le t\\le1$, and $f=t^4+2t^2$. Hence $2\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(t^5+2t^3)dt=4\\sqrt2/3$." "The speed is $2\\sqrt2\\,t$ on $0\\le t\\le1$, and $f=t^4+2t^2$. Hence $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(t^5+2t^3)dt=4\\sqrt2/3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484440 :multistep/context "Source question: Exercise **2.1.10**, [printed page 91 (PDF page 103)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=103).\n\nConsider\n\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\quad D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind each expression if possible; explain why if it is not possible.\n\nPart (h) continues on printed page 92 (PDF page 104)." "Source question: Exercise **2.1.10**, [printed page 91 (PDF page 103)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=103).\n\nConsider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\quad D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind each expression if possible; explain why if it is not possible.\n\nPart (h) continues on printed page 92 (PDF page 104)."] [:db/cas 17592186484452 :question/worked-solution "Parametrize $\\mathbf r(t,z)=(\\cos t,\\sin t,z)$ with $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$ and $0\\le z\\le\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t$. The norm of $\\mathbf r_t\\times\\mathbf r_z$ is 1. Hence the curved surface area is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t)dt=3\\pi$. The task asks for the cylinder wall, not its top or bottom caps." "Parametrize $\\mathbf r(t,z)=(\\cos t,\\sin t,z)$ with $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$ and $0\\le z\\le\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t$. The norm of $\\mathbf r_t\\times\\mathbf r_z$ is 1. Hence the curved surface area is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+2\\sin^2t)dt=3\\pi.\n$$ The task asks for the cylinder wall, not its top or bottom caps."] [:db/cas 17592186484458 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t-\\sin^2t\\,dt=0$. The field is the gradient of $xy$, so positive and negative tangential contributions cancel." "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t-\\sin^2t\\,dt=0.\n$$ The field is the gradient of $xy$, so positive and negative tangential contributions cancel."] [:db/cas 17592186484470 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} 1-\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=2\\pi$. The radial part contributes outward flow; the extra vertical component has zero net flux." "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} 1-\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=2\\pi.\n$$ The radial part contributes outward flow; the extra vertical component has zero net flux."] [:db/cas 17592186484492 :question/problem "**(a)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$.\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." "**(a)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds\n$$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds.\n$$\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484492 :question/worked-solution "Circulation: **positive**. Flux: **approximately zero**.\n\nThe arrows are upward along a path traversed from bottom to top in the right half-plane, so their tangential contribution is positive. The flux contributions on the lower and upper branches cancel: for the depicted vertical field with magnitude proportional to x, the right-normal flux is $-\\int x\\,dx$, whose endpoints have equal x-coordinates." "Circulation: **positive**. Flux: **approximately zero**.\n\nThe arrows are upward along a path traversed from bottom to top in the right half-plane, so their tangential contribution is positive. The flux contributions on the lower and upper branches cancel: for the depicted vertical field with magnitude proportional to x, the right-normal flux is $$\n\\displaystyle -\\int x\\,dx,\n$$ whose endpoints have equal x-coordinates."] [:db/cas 17592186484497 :question/problem "**(c)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$.\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." "**(c)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds\n$$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds.\n$$\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484505 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} 2\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0$. Inward and outward sectors cancel; the divergence is zero." "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With outward $\\mathbf n=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} 2\\sin t\\cos t\\,dt=0.\n$$ Inward and outward sectors cancel; the divergence is zero."] [:db/cas 17592186484510 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} -\\cos^2t\\,dt=-\\pi$. The radial part has no net circulation; the $-x$ vertical component produces a clockwise contribution." "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} -\\cos^2t\\,dt=-\\pi.\n$$ The radial part has no net circulation; the $-x$ vertical component produces a clockwise contribution."] [:db/cas 17592186484532 :multistep/context "Source question: Problem 1(a–c), PDF page 2.\n\nLet $C$ be the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$ and $f(x,y)=x^2+2y$. Calculate $\\int_C f\\,ds$, using $\\int_{-C} f\\,ds$ in part (c). The source states that scalar line integrals are independent of parametrization and orientation." "Source question: Problem 1(a–c), PDF page 2.\n\nLet $C$ be the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$ and $f(x,y)=x^2+2y$. Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C f\\,ds,\n$$ using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C} f\\,ds\n$$ in part (c). The source states that scalar line integrals are independent of parametrization and orientation."] [:db/cas 17592186484556 :question/worked-solution "Subtract row 1 from rows 2 and 3. This gives $y+4z=0$ and $-2y+2z=-1$, hence $10z=-1$, $z=-1/10$, $y=2/5$, and $x=6/5$. The RREF is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{-1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$. Substitution yields right-hand sides 2, 2, 1." "Subtract row 1 from rows 2 and 3. This gives $y+4z=0$ and $-2y+2z=-1$, hence $10z=-1$, $z=-1/10$, $y=2/5$, and $x=6/5$. The RREF is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{-1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substitution yields right-hand sides 2, 2, 1."] [:db/cas 17592186484566 :question/problem "**(a)** Calculate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." "**(a)** Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n$$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484566 :question/worked-solution "$\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=\\langle t^2+2t,t+t^3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\langle1,1\\rangle$. Thus $\\int_0^1(t^2+3t+t^3)dt=1/3+3/2+1/4=25/12$." "$\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=\\langle t^2+2t,t+t^3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\langle1,1\\rangle$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1(t^2+3t+t^3)dt=1/3+3/2+1/4=25/12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484567 :question/worked-solution "On $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, the outward normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. The flux is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}[a\\cos^2t+(b+c)\\sin t\\cos t+d\\sin^2t]dt=\\pi(a+d)$. Thus it is zero exactly when $a+d=0$, with b and c arbitrary. Independently, the divergence is $a+d$, so the flux form of Green’s theorem gives $(a+d)$ times the disk area $\\pi$." "On $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, the outward normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. The flux is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi}[a\\cos^2t+(b+c)\\sin t\\cos t+d\\sin^2t]dt=\\pi(a+d).\n$$ Thus it is zero exactly when $a+d=0$, with b and c arbitrary. Independently, the divergence is $a+d$, so the flux form of Green’s theorem gives $(a+d)$ times the disk area $\\pi$."] [:db/cas 17592186484580 :question/problem "**(b)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$.\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}." "**(b)** For the corresponding plotted curve, determine whether $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds\n$$ is positive, negative, or approximately zero. Do the same for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds.\n$$\n\nCirculation sign: {{circulation}}.\n\nFlux sign: {{flux}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484593 :question/worked-solution "The speed is $\\sqrt2$ and $f(\\mathbf r(t))=t^2+2t$. Thus $\\int_0^1(t^2+2t)\\sqrt2\\,dt=4\\sqrt2/3$." "The speed is $\\sqrt2$ and $f(\\mathbf r(t))=t^2+2t$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1(t^2+2t)\\sqrt2\\,dt=4\\sqrt2/3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484596 :question/problem "**(b)** Prove $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\int_C\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$. State the normal convention.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" "**(b)** Prove $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\int_C\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$ State the normal convention.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186484600 :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $\\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi$. Upward vectors on the right and downward vectors on the left align with CCW travel, giving positive circulation." "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. With $d\\mathbf r=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)dt$, the integral becomes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi.\n$$ Upward vectors on the right and downward vectors on the left align with CCW travel, giving positive circulation."] [:db/cas 17592186484611 :question/problem "**(a)** For the CCW circle of radius $a$ centered at the origin, calculate $\\int_Cx\\,dy$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/66/66c59485ae32ff038c4539f1e19950964319741bf7e642aca2a552c2a93a87c0.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." "**(a)** For the CCW circle of radius $a$ centered at the origin, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_Cx\\,dy\n$$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/66/66c59485ae32ff038c4539f1e19950964319741bf7e642aca2a552c2a93a87c0.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484611 :question/worked-solution "Use $x=a\\cos t,y=a\\sin t$. Then $\\oint x\\,dy=a^2\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi a^2$, equal to the disk area. The arrow directions give positive (CCW) orientation." "Use $x=a\\cos t,y=a\\sin t$. Then $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint x\\,dy=a^2\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2t\\,dt=\\pi a^2,\n$$ equal to the disk area. The arrow directions give positive (CCW) orientation."] [:db/cas 17592186484615 :question/problem "**(b)** For the plotted CCW square with opposite corners $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$, calculate $\\int_Cx\\,dy$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/fb/fbc268eb4de864e70cdf9bfcf0ce1b138631312f86ae3814516920c85df5e1c9.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." "**(b)** For the plotted CCW square with opposite corners $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_Cx\\,dy\n$$ and compare it with the enclosed area.\n\n![Oriented boundary and dimensions](images/fb/fbc268eb4de864e70cdf9bfcf0ce1b138631312f86ae3814516920c85df5e1c9.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484615 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal edges have $dy=0$. The right edge contributes $\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,dy=2$ and the left edge contributes $\\int_1^{-1}(-1)\\,dy=2$. Total 4, equal to the square’s area. Green’s theorem also gives $\\oint x\\,dy=\\iint_D1\\,dA$." "The horizontal edges have $dy=0$. The right edge contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,dy=2\n$$ and the left edge contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^{-1}(-1)\\,dy=2.\n$$ Total 4, equal to the square’s area. Green’s theorem also gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint x\\,dy=\\iint_D1\\,dA.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484624 :question/problem "Source question: Exercise **1.2.48**, [printed page 47 (PDF page 59)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=59).\n\nFind the rank of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$. Show row reduction by hand.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." "Source question: Exercise **1.2.48**, [printed page 47 (PDF page 59)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=59).\n\nFind the rank of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Show row reduction by hand.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484624 :question/worked-solution "The RREF is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, with pivots in columns 1, 3, 4. Rank is the number of pivots, hence 3. The fourth original row is the negative of the third, so rank cannot be 4." "The RREF is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ with pivots in columns 1, 3, 4. Rank is the number of pivots, hence 3. The fourth original row is the negative of the third, so rank cannot be 4."] [:db/cas 17592186484625 :question/problem "**(b)** Calculate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t^2\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}." "**(b)** Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n$$ using $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t^2\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Show the calculation.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186484773 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484781 :question/problem "$\\int \\sqrt{5x-50}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{5x-50}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484799 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484800 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484801 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484802 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484803 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485518 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix})\\}$" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix})\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186485522 :question/problem "${\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{7}÷{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{7}÷{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485542 :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=