)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186487391 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}Γ\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487393 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487395 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA,\n$$\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487395 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{4}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The integral is defined over a type II region $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{4}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487529 :question/problem "What is $\\int \\frac{12 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{12 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186487530 :question/problem "What is $\\int \\frac{10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186487536 :question/problem "What is $\\int \\frac{4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186487541 :question/problem "What is $\\int \\frac{6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186487549 :question/problem "What is $\\int \\frac{8 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{8 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186488188 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488189 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488246 :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186488350 :question/problem "$\\int (5{e}^{x}+{4}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{e}^{x}+{4}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488353 :question/problem "$\\int ({8}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({8}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488365 :question/problem "$\\int ({4}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({4}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488377 :question/problem "$\\int (2{e}^{x}-{7}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{e}^{x}-{7}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488408 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero." "If $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}},\n$$ find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero."] [:db/cas 17592186488413 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero." "If $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}},\n$$ find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero."] [:db/cas 17592186488417 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero." "If $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}},\n$$ find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero."] [:db/cas 17592186488419 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero." "If $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}},\n$$ find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero."] [:db/cas 17592186488433 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero." "If $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}},\n$$ find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero."] [:db/cas 17592186489187 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+4}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+4}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489203 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6x+5}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6x+5}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489229 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6x+5}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6x+5}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489231 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+6x+5}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+6x+5}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489240 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{7x-2}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{13}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{7x-2}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{13}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489241 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}-11x+15}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}-11x+15}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489256 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{5x-6}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{17}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{5x-6}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{17}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489259 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489260 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4x-5}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{19}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4x-5}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{19}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489275 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-4}{4x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{31}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-4}{4x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{31}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186489285 :question/worked-solution "The length of the remaining pipe is $(5\\,\\frac{1}{8}-3\\,\\frac{3}{4})\\text{ft}.$\n\nWriting $5\\,\\frac{1}{8}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{5\\times 8+1}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $8.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{15}{4}$ over a denominator of $8,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15\\times 2}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{30}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{30}{8}=\\frac{11}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{8}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,3=1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{ft}$ of pipe remains." "The length of the remaining pipe is $$\n\\displaystyle (5\\,\\frac{1}{8}-3\\,\\frac{3}{4})\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nWriting $5\\,\\frac{1}{8}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{5\\times 8+1}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $8.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{15}{4}$ over a denominator of $8,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15\\times 2}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{30}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{30}{8}=\\frac{11}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{8}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,3=1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{ft}$ of pipe remains."] [:db/cas 17592186489288 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x-1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x-1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489293 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{15x+4}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,86\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{15x+4}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,86\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186490212 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)i+\\left(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)j$ over the domain $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.$" "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)i+\\left(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\right)j\n$$ over the domain $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186490217 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since $A$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have that $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 2+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\"" "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since $A$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have that $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 2+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186490224 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(8-10)^{-3}=\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(-2)^{-3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(-2)^{-3}=\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(-2)^{-3} &= \\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\times \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{5\\times 3}{8\\times 1}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{8}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{15+1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{16}{8} \\\\ &= 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(8-10)^{-3}=\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(-2)^{-3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle (-2)^{-3}=\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(-2)^{-3} &= \\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\times \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{5\\times 3}{8\\times 1}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{8}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{15+1}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{16}{8} \\\\ &= 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490225 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490242 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490244 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Given the **orthonormal** basis $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186490258 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490316 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{6} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has $n=6$ columns and $2$ of them are non-pivot ones, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6-2=4\\ne 6.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=4$,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=6$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{6} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has $n=6$ columns and $2$ of them are non-pivot ones, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6-2=4\\ne 6.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=4$,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=6$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186490321 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490328 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\beg